  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nA potent shortwave will be exiting the area Friday. In it's \nwake a cold front will have crossed the area with gusty north winds \nexpected through the day. Cooler temperatures will be experienced \nbehind this system, especially across portions of north central \nNebraska, where highs in the lower 80s are forecast. Drier air will \nwork into the area behind the front, with lows in the comfortable \nupper 50s to around 60 degrees Friday night. \n\nOn Saturday, expect the upper level ridging to expand north and \neastward from the desert southwest. This will begin a warm-up region \nwide. Highs will range from 4 to 7 degrees above normal across \nwestern Nebraska, with near normal readings across central into \nnorth central Nebraska. This puts actual highs ranging from the mid \n80s across the central/north to lower to mid 90s west.  \n\nUpper level ridging continues to expand across the area Sunday. This \nwill continue to bring more warmth with highs exceeding 90 degrees \nat all locations. Deepening low pressure across Wyoming ahead of a \nbroad long wave trough across the northern Rockies will bring \nstrong, gusty south winds particularly west of Highway 183. Highs in \nthese areas will be well into the 90s with humidity likely dropping \ninto the 20-25% range by afternoon across the Panhandle and far \nwestern Nebraska. This will bring an increase in fire weather \nconcerns, especially for areas that haven't received rain and much \ngreen-up.  \n\nEarly next week the ridge may flatten some and along with higher \nPWAT values. This could bring a return to some chance for rainfall. \nStill could see some fire weather concerns yet Monday, but these \ndecrease Tuesday as temperatures cool back some and humidity rises.",
